        {
          "id": "the_collapsed_base_rate_reproduces_both_of_the_references_generator_tables",
          "description": "`rtDay_meanBaseRate` IS `baserate_1_2020.meanBaseRate` on the 9101 rows and `baserate_9_2020.meanBaseRate` on the 11609 rows -- the reference's own S14 intermediates, computed here. This is the most direct check of W available, and the brakewear half is the check this fixture adds: the base rate factorises exactly around W, the two rate paths share the same 46 weights, and the two rate tables are seven orders of magnitude apart -- 333,651 kJ per source-hour against 0.0600 g -- so a wrong W is a wrong number in every one of these cells and no shared scale factor can hide behind it.\n\nSIXTEEN ASSERTIONS BECAME THIRTEEN AT THE DAY CORRECTION (docs/esm-conventions.md §42). Each rate row used to be asserted at BOTH day types and is now asserted at the one the run selects; the weekday numbers are the reference's own and were mostly already here, and the four that were only ever asserted at the weekend -- rate rows 41 and 88 on 9101, 369 and 416 on 11609 -- were re-read out of `baserate_1_2020` and `baserate_9_2020` at hourDayID 95 rather than rescaled from the weekend ones.\n\n`rtDay_meanBaseRateACAdj` is the INCREMENTAL A/C load of sbweighted.rs:459. It reaches emissionQuant multiplied by an A/C factor this document computes to be exactly 0, so the OUTPUT cannot see it -- but the generator tables carry it in their own column, so it is checked here anyway, on both paths: 26,899.6 for 9101 and an exact 0 for 11609, which is what an absent `fullacadjustment` row has to produce.\n\nThe tolerance is the reference's six-significant-figure column storage.",
